Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions
Issue: Users cannot log in after creation

Solution: Verify password complexity requirements are met
Check that the user account is active (not disabled)
Ensure correct username and password are being used
Issue: Users don't see expected resources

Solution: Verify team membership is correctly configured
Check that permissions are properly assigned to teams
Ensure resources are associated with the correct organization
Issue: Permission denied errors

Solution: Review team permissions and ensure appropriate access levels
Check that users are members of the correct teams
Verify resource-level permissions are configured
Issue: Teams not showing expected permissions

Solution: Refresh the browser page
Log out and log back in to refresh user session
Check that permissions were saved correctly
Verification Commands
To verify user creation from the command line (if SSH access is available):

# Check if users exist in the system
sudo -u awx awx-manage list_users

# Verify team memberships
sudo -u awx awx-manage list_teams
Best Practices Implemented
Throughout this lab, we've implemented several security and management best practices:

• Principle of Least Privilege: Users are granted only the minimum permissions necessary for their roles • Role-Based Access Control: Permissions are assigned to teams rather than individual users • Clear Naming Conventions: Usernames and team names clearly indicate their purpose • Strong Password Requirements: All accounts use complex passwords • Separation of Duties: Different teams have different levels of access to resources

Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

• Created multiple users representing different organizational roles (developers, operations staff, and managers) • Organized users into teams based on their job functions and responsibilities • Configured appropriate permissions for each team to ensure proper access control • Tested user access to verify that security controls work as expected • Implemented role-based access control following industry best practices

Why This Matters: Proper user and group management is critical for maintaining security and operational efficiency in automation environments. By organizing users into teams with appropriate permissions, you ensure that:

Security is maintained through controlled access to sensitive automation resources
Operational efficiency is improved by giving users access to the tools they need
Compliance requirements are met through proper access controls and audit trails
Scalability is achieved by managing permissions at the team level rather than individual users
This foundation in user and group management will be essential as you prepare for the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Managing Automation with Ansible Automation Platform exam and in real-world automation deployments where proper access control is crucial for both security and operational success.

The skills you've developed in this lab directly apply to enterprise environments where multiple teams collaborate on automation projects while maintaining appropriate security boundaries and access controls.
